<p class="eyebrow">Schemas / Migration</p>

<h1>Migrating from v0.3 to v0.4</h1>

<p class="lede">
  You only need to migrate a document if you want its v0.4 lifecycle fields.
  Existing v0.3 documents keep working and stay valid against the v0.3
  contracts. For each document you do move, the work is small: point
  <code>$schema</code> at the v0.4 URL, set <code>schemaVersion</code> to
  <code>"0.4.0"</code>, and rewrite any lifecycle metadata in the new shape.
</p>

<h2 id="start">Start here</h2>

<p>
  v0.4 is a lifecycle release. It standardizes a canonical
  <code>status</code> field across every manifest entity, centralizes
  deprecation records in a new shared
  <a href="/docs/schemas/v0.4/lifecycle/">lifecycle fragment</a>, accepts an
  explicit <code>deprecated: false</code> assertion in native manifests, and
  moves every contract URL from the v0.3 <code>/schemas/v0.3/…</code> addresses to
  <code>/schemas/v0.4/…</code>. Existing v0.3 documents remain valid against the
  v0.3 contracts: nothing is converted automatically, and migrating is an
  explicit, document-by-document or profile-by-profile decision.
</p>

<aside class="schema-install" aria-label="Package imports">
  <p class="schema-install__kicker">Package imports</p>
  <h2>Install the contracts</h2>
  <p>
    Both formats ship in the same npm package. Pin the package release
    (<code>0.4.0</code>) for installation; use the format version
    (<code>v0.4</code>) in schema URLs and import paths.
  </p>
  <dl class="schema-install__sources">
    <div>
      <dt>npm</dt>
      <dd><code>npm install @designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>v0.4 import (migrated documents)</dt>
      <dd><code>import schema from '@designlasagna/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json' assert { type: 'json' };</code></dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>v0.3 import (unmigrated documents)</dt>
      <dd><code>import schema from '@designlasagna/schemas/v0.3/tokens.json' assert { type: 'json' };</code></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</aside>

<h2 id="format-vs-package">Format version <code>v0.4</code> vs package release <code>0.4.0</code></h2>

<p>
  <strong><code>v0.4</code> is the schema format version</strong> — the set of
  contracts under <code>/schemas/v0.4/</code> and the v0.4 section of these
  docs. <strong><code>0.4.0</code> is the npm release version</strong> of
  <code>@designlasagna/schemas</code> that ships those files
  (<code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code>, import paths
  <code>@designlasagna/schemas/v0.4/*.json</code>).
</p>

<p>
  The two move together but are different things, so keep them straight:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    In a manifest's <code>$schema</code>, use the format version in the URL:
    <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    In a native manifest's <code>schemaVersion</code> field (a plain semver
    string), write <code>"0.4.0"</code> for a v0.4-format manifest —
    <code>"0.3.0"</code> for one that stays on v0.3.
  </li>
  <li>
    When installing or importing contracts, pin the package release:
    <code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code>.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  See the <a href="/docs/schemas/">schema overview</a> for the same
  format-vs-release distinction.
</p>

<h2 id="lifecycle-selection">How lifecycle selection works</h2>

<p>
  v0.4 lifecycle fields are opt-in: per document for native manifests
  (via <code>$schema</code> and <code>schemaVersion</code>), and per
  consuming profile for CEM and DTCG extensions. There is no automatic,
  lossless conversion of legacy data, and you should not assume universal
  v0.4 consumer support — check the target consumer's support before
  emitting v0.4 documents.
</p>

<h3>Native manifests (tokens, utilities, icons)</h3>

<p>
  A native manifest declares its format through its <code>$schema</code> URL
  and <code>schemaVersion</code>. The URL selects the validating contract;
  <code>schemaVersion</code> is validated as a plain string, not a
  version-dispatch algorithm, so the consuming resolver selects the matching
  v0.4 profile itself:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    A v0.3 manifest keeps its v0.3 <code>$schema</code> URL and
    <code>schemaVersion: "0.3.0"</code>. It remains valid against the v0.3
    contract; no re-validation or reinterpretation happens.
  </li>
  <li>
    To migrate, set <code>$schema</code> to the v0.4 URL (for example
    <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json</code>) and
    <code>schemaVersion: "0.4.0"</code>. The document is then valid only
    against the v0.4 contract, and a v0.4-capable consumer is required.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>CEM and DTCG (host formats)</h3>

<p>
  CEM manifests and DTCG token files are standard host formats, so there is no
  Design Lasagna <code>schemaVersion</code> to change:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    A CEM manifest keeps its standard CEM <code>schemaVersion</code>. Never
    replace it with a Design Lasagna version.
  </li>
  <li>
    DTCG files stay standard DTCG; lifecycle metadata lives in
    <code>$extensions["recipes.designlasagna"]</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    The lifecycle fields inside CEM extensions and DTCG extensions become
    active when the consuming project or resolver config selects lifecycle
    profile <code>0.4</code>. Without that selection, the legacy profile
    applies: existing consumers keep parsing documents exactly as before, and
    no v0.4 lifecycle rules are applied to the new fields. That selection
    mechanism is
    documented in the package's lifecycle documentation (<code>docs/lifecycle.md</code>
    inside <code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code>); no schema file encodes
    it.
  </li>
  <li>
    During the transition, keep dual support: v0.3 and v0.4 documents coexist,
    and each validates against its own format's contracts.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  Legacy parser-only forms — native booleans/strings, raw DTCG
  <code>$status</code>/<code>$removal</code>/<code>$replacement</code>, or
  prose-derived data — have no lossless upgrade path. Ask authors for
  structured <code>message</code>, <code>removal</code>, and
  <code>replacement</code> values instead of guessing them.
</p>

<p>
  One infrastructure note: the v0.4 contracts reference the shared
  <a href="/docs/schemas/v0.4/lifecycle/">lifecycle fragment</a> with relative
  <code>$ref</code>s. A validator that fetches only a single file will fail to
  resolve them — register all six exported v0.4 schemas by <code>$id</code>
  (as this site's validation setup does) when validating offline.
</p>

<h2 id="per-contract">What changed per contract</h2>

<div class="schema-table-wrap">
  <table class="schema-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Contract</th>
        <th>v0.3 → v0.4 headline</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tokens.json</code></td>
        <td><code>status</code> added; <code>deprecated</code> accepts an explicit <code>false</code>; platform mappings closed (<code>usage</code> removed)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>utilities.json</code></td>
        <td><code>deprecated</code> accepts an explicit <code>false</code>; <code>status</code> now references the shared definition</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>icons.json</code></td>
        <td><code>status</code> added; <code>deprecated</code> accepts an explicit <code>false</code>; first v0.4 format with an icons reference page</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cem-extensions.json</code></td>
        <td>Standard <code>deprecated</code> marker modeled in the lifecycle fields; <code>status</code> now references the shared definition; per-entity extension definitions added</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>dtcg-extensions.json</code></td>
        <td><code>status</code> added to token and group extensions; replacement semantics made explicit; platform mappings closed</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lifecycle.json</code></td>
        <td>New shared definitions-only fragment (no v0.3 counterpart)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>
  All five existing contracts also move their <code>$id</code>/<code>$schema</code>
  from <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/v0.3/&lt;file&gt;</code> to
  <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/schemas/v0.4/&lt;file&gt;</code>.
</p>
<h3>tokens.json</h3>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>New optional <code>Token.status</code></strong>
    (<code>$ref</code> <code>Status</code>). The v0.3 token definition had no
    <code>status</code> field.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>Token.deprecated</code> is now the shared
    <code>NativeDeprecation</code> union</strong>: <code>false</code>,
    <code>null</code>, or a <code>Deprecated</code> record. v0.3 allowed only
    <code>null</code> or a record — the explicit <code>false</code> assertion
    (the author asserts "not deprecated", as distinct from omitted) is new.
    <code>null</code> still means "not asserted".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Platform mappings are closed.</strong> The <code>usage</code>
    string is removed and <code>additionalProperties</code> is now
    <code>false</code>, so v0.3 documents with custom platform keys or
    <code>usage</code> values are no longer valid — drop or rehome that data.
  </li>
  <li>
    The local <code>Deprecated</code> definition is now an alias of the shared
    lifecycle definition: same fields (<code>message</code> required,
    <code>removal</code>, <code>replacement</code> optional), but
    <code>message</code> must now be a non-empty string.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>utilities.json</h3>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong><code>UtilityClass.status</code> is unchanged in accepted
    values.</strong> v0.3 already accepted any string; v0.4 now references the
    shared <code>Status</code> definition, giving <code>deprecated</code> and
    <code>removed</code> normative meaning.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>UtilityClass.deprecated</code> is now
    <code>NativeDeprecation</code></strong> — the explicit
    <code>false</code> branch is new, exactly as for tokens.
  </li>
  <li>
    The local <code>Deprecated</code> definition is now an alias of the shared
    lifecycle definition (non-empty <code>message</code> required).
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>icons.json</h3>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>New optional <code>Icon.status</code></strong>
    (<code>$ref</code> <code>Status</code>). The v0.3 icon definition had no
    <code>status</code> field.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>Icon.deprecated</code> is now <code>NativeDeprecation</code></strong>
    — the explicit <code>false</code> branch is new.
  </li>
  <li>
    The local <code>Deprecated</code> definition is now an alias of the shared
    lifecycle definition (non-empty <code>message</code> required).
  </li>
  <li>
    v0.4 is the first format version with an
    <a href="/docs/schemas/v0.4/icons/">icons reference page</a> on this site;
    the v0.3 <code>icons.json</code> contract was published as a raw file
    (<a href="/schemas/v0.3/icons.json"><code>/schemas/v0.3/icons.json</code></a>)
    without a reference page.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>cem-extensions.json</h3>

<p>
  Both versions are definitions-only fragments composed against a pinned CEM
  schema; neither validates a CEM manifest on its own.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong><code>LifecycleFields.deprecated</code> is new:</strong> the
    standard CEM deprecation marker (boolean, or string carrying the
    human-readable message), now modeled as part of the lifecycle extension.
    v0.3's <code>LifecycleFields</code> had only <code>status</code>,
    <code>removal</code>, and <code>replacement</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>LifecycleFields.status</code> now references the shared
    <code>Status</code> definition.</strong> v0.3 accepted any string; v0.4
    still accepts any string, with <code>deprecated</code> and
    <code>removed</code> given normative meaning.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>removal</code> and <code>replacement</code> semantics are
    clarified:</strong> removal targets are an ISO date
    (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>), a semver (<code>X.Y.Z</code>), or a quarter
    (<code>YYYY-Qn</code>); replacement is scoped to the same CEM entity kind
    and owner.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Per-entity extension definitions:</strong> new
    <code>DeclarationExtensions</code>, <code>MemberExtensions</code>,
    <code>CssPartExtensions</code>, and <code>EventExtensions</code> (each a
    <code>$ref</code> to <code>LifecycleFields</code>);
    <code>SlotExtensions</code> and <code>CssPropertyExtensions</code> now
    reference <code>LifecycleFields</code> instead of duplicating the fields
    inline, gaining <code>status</code> in the process — v0.3's inline forms
    only had <code>deprecated</code>, <code>removal</code>, and
    <code>replacement</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>AttributeExtensions</code> gains <code>deprecated</code></strong>
    (boolean or string) and <code>status</code> (<code>$ref</code>
    <code>Status</code>); <code>enum</code> and <code>deprecatedValues</code>
    are unchanged.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>DeprecatedValue</code> is now an alias</strong> of
    <code>lifecycle.json#/definitions/DeprecatedValue</code> — the same
    fields (<code>value</code> and <code>message</code> required,
    <code>removal</code>/<code>replacement</code> optional), with
    <code>message</code> now required to be non-empty.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>dtcg-extensions.json</h3>

<p>
  Both versions are definitions-only fragments for
  <code>$extensions["recipes.designlasagna"]</code> on standard DTCG files.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>New optional <code>status</code></strong> on
    <code>TokenExtensions</code> and <code>GroupExtensions</code>
    (<code>$ref</code> <code>Status</code>). v0.3 had no <code>status</code>
    field in either extension.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Group deprecation replacement semantics are explicit:</strong>
    the nearest declaration replaces the whole inherited record (no
    field-level merging), and the standard DTCG <code>$deprecated: false</code>
    clears inherited deprecation.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>Deprecated</code> is now an alias of the shared
    <code>StrictDeprecated</code></strong> — the same closed record v0.3 used
    (<code>message</code> required, <code>removal</code> and
    <code>replacement</code> optional, no unknown keys), with a non-empty
    <code>message</code> required.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Platform mappings are closed.</strong> The <code>usage</code>
    string is removed and <code>additionalProperties</code> is now
    <code>false</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Both extension objects remain closed (<code>additionalProperties:
    false</code>) — <code>status</code> is the only field added to either.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="lifecycle-fragment">The new lifecycle fragment</h2>

<p>
  v0.4 adds <a href="/schemas/v0.4/lifecycle.json"><code>lifecycle.json</code></a>
  — a definitions-only, composable fragment (not a manifest validator) that
  the other v0.4 contracts reference with relative <code>$ref</code>s. See the
  <a href="/docs/schemas/v0.4/lifecycle/">lifecycle reference page</a> for the
  full field guide.
</p>

<div class="schema-table-wrap">
  <table class="schema-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Definition</th>
        <th>Shape</th>
        <th>Used by</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>Status</code></td>
        <td>Any string; only <code>deprecated</code> and <code>removed</code> carry normative meaning</td>
        <td>Every v0.4 contract's <code>status</code> field</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>Deprecated</code></td>
        <td>Permissive record: <code>message</code> required (non-empty), <code>removal</code>, <code>replacement</code> optional</td>
        <td>tokens, utilities, icons (via <code>NativeDeprecation</code>)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>StrictDeprecated</code></td>
        <td>Same fields, but closed (no unknown keys)</td>
        <td>dtcg-extensions</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>NativeDeprecation</code></td>
        <td>Union: <code>false</code> | <code>null</code> | <code>Deprecated</code></td>
        <td>tokens, utilities, icons <code>deprecated</code> fields</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>DeprecatedValue</code></td>
        <td>Per-value record: <code>value</code> and <code>message</code> (non-empty) required, <code>removal</code>, <code>replacement</code> optional</td>
        <td>cem-extensions <code>deprecatedValues</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>
  In v0.3 the deprecation record shapes lived inline, duplicated per
  contract; v0.4 gives every lifecycle definition a single canonical home.
</p>

<h2 id="unchanged">What did not change</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Root manifest requirements:</strong> tokens still require
    <code>schemaVersion</code> and <code>tokens</code>; utilities still
    require <code>schemaVersion</code>; icons still require
    <code>schemaVersion</code> and <code>icons</code>. <code>schemaVersion</code>
    remains a plain string.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Extension namespaces:</strong> the CEM <code>$extensions</code>
    key and the DTCG <code>recipes.designlasagna</code> key are unchanged.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Deprecated record core:</strong> <code>message</code> is still
    required, and <code>removal</code>/<code>replacement</code> remain
    optional strings.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Utilities <code>status</code>:</strong> still accepts any string
    (now canonical, not constrained).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Old documents stay valid:</strong> v0.3 documents remain valid
    against the v0.3 contracts, and v0.2 contracts are untouched.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="checklist">Migration checklist</h2>

<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Inventory.</strong> List your v0.3 documents (tokens, utilities,
    icons manifests; CEM manifests; DTCG files with
    <code>recipes.designlasagna</code> extensions) and the consumers of each.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Choose what moves.</strong> Migrate only documents whose consumers
    support v0.4: native documents need a v0.4-capable consumer, and
    CEM/DTCG lifecycle fields are read only where the consuming project or
    resolver config explicitly selects lifecycle profile <code>0.4</code>.
    Everything else stays on v0.3 — the v0.3 contracts remain published and
    valid.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Native manifests.</strong> Point <code>$schema</code> at the v0.4
    URL (for example
    <code>https://designlasagna.recipes/schemas/v0.4/tokens.json</code>) and
    set <code>schemaVersion: "0.4.0"</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Rewrite lifecycle metadata.</strong> Add <code>status</code> where
    relevant; convert <code>deprecated</code> to the record shape with a
    non-empty <code>message</code> plus <code>removal</code> and
    <code>replacement</code>. Use <code>deprecated: false</code> only where an
    explicit negative assertion is wanted. There is no automatic conversion of
    legacy parser-only values — ask authors for structured data.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Drop removed data.</strong> Remove <code>PlatformMapping.usage</code>
    (tokens and DTCG extensions) and any custom platform keys in platform
    mappings (both are closed now).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>CEM manifests.</strong> Keep the standard CEM
    <code>schemaVersion</code>; author <code>status</code> and
    <code>deprecated</code> in the extension fields; select profile
    <code>0.4</code> in the consuming project or resolver config.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>DTCG files.</strong> Keep the standard <code>$deprecated</code>
    boolean/string mirror; put the structured record in
    <code>recipes.designlasagna</code>; add <code>status</code> where useful;
    select profile <code>0.4</code> in the resolver config.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Tooling.</strong> Install <code>@designlasagna/schemas@0.4.0</code>
    (or a later 0.4.x), import from the <code>v0.4/</code> paths, and register
    all six v0.4 schemas by <code>$id</code> for offline validation.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Validate both directions.</strong> Migrated documents validate
    against the v0.4 contracts; unmigrated documents continue to validate
    against v0.3.
  </li>
</ol>

